Ladybird takes a flex container’s first baseline from its startmost
flex line and its last baseline from its endmost one, which under
wrap-reverse are its last and first lines. Gecko’s DoFlexLayout() picks
the same lines through StartmostLine() and EndmostLine(), which check
IsCrossAxisReversed(). WebKit’s FlexLayout::flexItemForFirstBaseline()
and flexItemForLastBaseline() swap the first and last lines for
wrap-reverse too. And Blink’s FlexLayoutAlgorithm::ApplyReversals()
reverses the lines for wrap-reverse before its BaselineAccumulator reads
the baselines of the first and last lines.

Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5 agree on five
cases: a reversed row with wrap-reverse and mixed order values, a
reversed row and a row with mixed order values, a reversed row with
equal ones, and a row with wrap-reverse. Ladybird now matches all five.
